.TH BX 1 "" bx "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bx \- Bash eXecutor, a configurable build automation and recipe invocation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbx\fR [\fB\-f\fR \fIbashfile\fR] [options] [\fB\-\-\fR] [recipe ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The
.I bx
utility is a build automation and recipe invocation tool that runs entirely in
Bash.  It reads a configuration file called a
.IR Bashfile ,
treats Bash functions declared in it as
.IR recipes ,
and invokes them by name:
.PP
.B bx
.I recipe
.PP
Any valid Bash 3.2+ code may be used in a
.IR Bashfile ,
so recipes may use the full power of the shell.  A
recipe declared in the file
.I Bashfile
as follows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
lint() {
  shellcheck "src/*.sh"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
is invoked with
.PP
.RS
.B bx
.I lint
.RE
.PP
A recipe is a set of instructions declared in a
.I Bashfile
as a Bash function.  Private functions, prefixed by
.B _
by convention, are not treated as recipes.
.PP
When no recipe is given,
.I bx
invokes the default recipe, marked with the
.B @default
annotation (see ANNOTATIONS below).  If no default recipe is declared,
.I bx
fails with an error.
.PP
The
.I Bashfile
may be specified explicitly with the
.B \-f
option.  Otherwise,
.I bx
looks for the nearest
.I Bashfile
by walking up the directory tree from the current working directory (see FILES
below).  This allows recipes to be invoked from any subdirectory of a project.
.SH OPTIONS
.sp 1
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR=\fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-bashfile\fR=\fIfile\fR
Read
.I file
as a bashfile instead of searching for the default
.IR Bashfile .
Only one bashfile may be specified.
.TP 0.5i
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Display the usage line, the list of options, and the available recipes,
together with any recipe descriptions declared with the
.B @help
annotation.
.TP 0.5i
.BR \-l , " \-\-list"
Display the available recipes, together with any recipe descriptions
declared with the
.B @help
annotation.
.TP 0.5i
.BR \-q , " \-\-quiet"
Do not display the invoked recipe traces, nor the xtrace output.  This is
useful when the recipes themselves print the desired output.
.TP 0.5i
.BR \-v , " \-\-version"
Display the version of
.I bx
and exit without requiring a
.IR Bashfile .
.TP 0.5i
.B \-\-
Treat all remaining arguments as recipes, even if they begin with a hyphen.
.SH RECIPES
.PP
.I bx
may invoke several recipes in a single execution, in the order given:
.PP
.RS
.B bx
.I format
.I lint
.RE
.PP
Arguments may be passed to a recipe by quoting the recipe name together with
its arguments.  They are made available to the recipe as
.IR $1 ,
.I $2 ,
etc:
.PP
.RS
.B bx
.IR "'lint --some-lint-option'"
.RE
.PP
A recipe that has already been invoked may legitimately be invoked again with
different arguments.  However, when a recipe is already in the middle of being
invoked, directly or indirectly, and is invoked again on the same invocation
path, the re\-invocation is skipped with a warning.  This prevents circular
invocation.
.SS "Nested invocation"
.PP
A recipe may invoke other recipes at runtime, without spawning a separate
.B bx
process, using the
.B bx::invoke
helper:
.PP
.RS
.nf
complex-recipe() {
  echo "Pre-processing..."
  bx::invoke simple-recipe
  echo "Post-processing..."
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B bx::invoke
helper accepts the same syntax as the command line: several recipes may be
passed, and arguments are forwarded to the invoked recipe.
.SS "Scope boundaries"
.PP
Recipes declared with
.B ()
(subshell) run in their own scope: if they modify global variables, the
changes are not visible to other recipes.  Recipes declared with
.B {}
(functions) share the parent scope: if they modify global variables, the
changes are visible to other recipes.
.SS "Shell options isolation"
.PP
Shell options, such as
.B set
.BI \-x
for tracing, are isolated locally.  Changes made inside a recipe do not leak
into other recipes, nor into
.I bx
itself.
.SH ANNOTATIONS
.PP
Annotations are directives placed inside a recipe body.  They are read by
.I bx
when loading the
.I Bashfile
and instruct it on how to treat the recipe.
.TP 0.5i
.B @help
.I "description ..."
Associate a short description with the recipe.  The description is displayed
by
.B bx
.B \-l
and
.B bx
.BR \-h ,
and may span multiple lines by passing several arguments:
.PP
.RS
.nf
my-recipe() {
  @help "Line 1" \\
    "Line 2"
}
.fi
.RE
.TP 0.5i
.B @default
Mark the recipe as the default recipe, invoked when
.B bx
is executed without an explicit recipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
my-recipe() {
  @default
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
At most one recipe may be marked as the default.  A default recipe must not be
a private function (a name prefixed by
.BR _ ).
.TP 0.5i
.B @confirm
Mark the recipe as requiring interactive confirmation before execution.  When
the recipe is about to be invoked,
.I bx
prompts the user with a
.BI "[y/N] "
question on standard error and waits for a single\-character reply.  Pressing
.B y
confirms the invocation; any other input (including an empty reply) cancels
it and aborts the chain.
.PP
.RS
.nf
dangerous-recipe() {
  @confirm

  rm -rf ./build
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The prompt is shown for every matching recipe in a chain, including recipes
invoked through nested invocation via
.BR bx::invoke .
.SH FILES
.TP 0.5i
.I Bashfile
The default configuration file read by
.IR bx .
When the
.B \-f
option is not given,
.I bx
searches for the nearest
.I Bashfile
starting from the current working directory and walking up its parents, until
one is found or the root directory is reached.  If none is found,
.I bx
fails, unless
.B \-h
or
.B \-v
was requested.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
.I bx
exits with a status of zero if all invoked recipes completed successfully.  A
nonzero status is returned if any recipe, or
.I bx
itself, encountered an error.  Error messages are printed to standard error
with the
.B bx:
prefix.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR bash (1)
and the
.I bx
project documentation.
